WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.162
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:10.140
Take a moment and picture your favorite
childhood bicycle.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:12.530
What did it look like?

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.420
What color was it?

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.520
Did you have one of those weird banana
seats that used to be so popular?

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.560
Or perhaps you were the creative type with
cards in your spokes and

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.710
streamers coming from your handlebar.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:29.440
My bike was a bright red Schwinn with a
cushy black seat and

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.985
these sweet pegs on my front wheel.

00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:34.280
[LAUGH] I rode my bike everywhere as a
child,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.050
to school, to my friend's house, to the
pool.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.685
I even rode it on an ill-fated attempt to
run away from home.

00:00:41.685 --> 00:00:48.010
[LAUGH] You see as children, without a car
or a drivers license, it is

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.820
the bike that provides with the ultimate
sense of freedom and independence.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.520
Now think back to your most recent
interaction with a bike.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:57.800
When was it?

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:00.030
What were you doing?

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.490
Perhaps you commuted to campus today on a
bike, or maybe you went for

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.840
a ride on the curvy roads of coastal
California.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.590
As adults, the bike provides us with a
different type of freedom.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:16.300
Freedom from work, freedom from stress,
freedom from routine.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.310
The bike scratches our itch for adventure,
and

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.100
pri, provides us with a brief escape from
reality.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.550
I've been riding bikes my entire life.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.720
For a period of time, I even raced
mountain bikes professionally,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:29.510
on the World Cup circuit.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.720
It's easy for many of us to view the bike
through the lens of a child,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.090
a weekend warrior, or maybe even a
professional.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.898
But a few years ago, I began to wonder if
there's something more to the bike.

00:01:40.898 --> 00:01:44.080
What if that freedom that a bike provides
is more impactful than just

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.260
the independence of a child?

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.330
Or the exercise of an adult?

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.680
What if the bike could inspire us to
tackle some of the world's most

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.340
complex problems?

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:55.729
Allow me to take you on a bit of a
journey.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.090
It's July 2009 in a tiny village in rural
Zambia.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.230
Belita, a 15 year old girl who lives in
the village,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.540
wakes up before dawn to feed her family's
chickens before school.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.420
Belita commutes daily to Chalala Basic
school.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.589
Three miles away over unmaintained dirt
roads.

00:02:17.875 --> 00:02:22.070
Belita, unlikely many of us, Belita does
not have access to a car or a school bus.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.710
So Belita has to make this long walk on
foot.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.790
Now let's think about this for a second.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.180
Three miles, on foot, every day, each
direction.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:38.920
That's like walking from this classroom to
the Dutch Goose, twice a day,

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.780
barefoot on dirt roads on the hottest day
of the year in Palo Alto.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.980
I also don't think there is a juicy
hamburger and

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.150
GSP phone at the end of this road.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.890
But seriously, how good would your school
attendance be if you

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.120
had to walk that far to get to school?

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.070
I'll have to admit, I don't think mine
would be very good.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:01.280
On this particular day, it takes Belita 90
minutes to get to school.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.190
By the times she arrives, she is late, and
she's fatigued from the long commute.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.660
Belita didn't have time that morning to
complete her homework before school, so

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:11.700
she quickly falls behind.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.440
Belita attends school only three days a
week.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.910
Her academic performance is low.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.970
Now let me ask you.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:22.520
What do you think would happen if Belita,
if we gave Belita a bike?

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:28.660
Fast forward to 2011 in the same tiny
village in rural Zambia.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.750
Belita wakes up before dawn to feed her
family's chickens before school.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:36.630
Belita then collects her new bike

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.130
that she recently received from World
Bicycle Relief,

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:44.180
a charity organization that delivers
purpose-built bikes in places like Zambia.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.480
The three-mile commute to, to school takes
only 20 minutes on a bike.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.220
So Belita arrives on time, prepared, with
energy to focus.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.130
Belita now attends school five days a week
and her academic performance is soaring.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.910
The bike has reinvigorated Belita's energy
towards life and

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.650
she's now on track to achieving her life
long dream of becoming a nurse.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.160
You know,

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.730
Scientific American once published a
really great study that determined which

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:16.310
animal species consumed the least energy
to travel a fixed distance.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.140
Point a to point b.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.510
We're talking efficiency of motion.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.630
Perhaps not surprisingly, the condor,

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:26.900
with its ability to soar on its huge
ten-foot wingspan, ranked number one.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.290
The human strolled across the finish line.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.380
An underwhelming third of the way down the
list.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.960
Turns out we're not designed for speed or
efficiency.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.730
We are, however, quite adept at overcoming
our deficiencies using our brains.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.980
Researchers then reran the study providing
the human with a single tool.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.630
You got it, a bike.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.890
With the aid of a bike, the human rose all
the way to the top of the list,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:58.200
above the condor, as the most efficient
animal species on the planet.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.080
Behold, human creativity and the power of
the bicycle.

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:08.110
My friends, the bike is an incredibly
powerful tool.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:12.060
With the aid of a bicycle a human can
carry five times as much weight and

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.070
can travel four times faster than a person
on foot.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.970
That trip to the market that once yielded
40 pounds of rice now yields 200.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.960
That hour and a half commute is now 20
minutes.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.080
These statistics are staggering.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.623
Staggering enough to be life changing for
Belita and

00:05:28.623 --> 00:05:31.820
others who do not have access to modern
forms of transportation.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:34.130
Studies have proven,

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:38.220
that in places like Zambia, a bike can
increase academic performance by 60%.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:44.581
That's the difference between a GMAT score
of 440, and a GMAT score of 700.

00:05:44.581 --> 00:05:50.165
[LAUGH] I don't need to get the dean down
here to tell you how meaningful that is.

00:05:50.165 --> 00:05:55.140
[LAUGH]
The bike has the power

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.790
to free people from poverty, now that is
what I call freedom.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.320
But not only is the bike a really powerful
tool, it's also a very simple tool.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.940
Two wheels, pedals, handlebar, and a seat,
connected by a simple metal frame.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.560
You can even take a class here at Stanford
where you learn to build your very own

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:12.230
bike from scratch.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.610
And the bike is old technology.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.790
The bike was invented in Germany in 1817
and

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:21.590
much of the original construction has
remained unchanged in the last 200 years.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:26.670
Look, I don't mean to suggest that the
bike is without its challenges.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.660
We need to fix flat tires.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:30.540
And repair used bikes.

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:35.390
But if we can overcome these small
hurdles, we have such an incredible tool.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:39.750
A bike is powerful, and yet it's simple.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.732
A bike has the ability to meaningfully
improve huge global problems like

00:06:45.732 --> 00:06:47.859
illiteracy, poverty, and hunger.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:53.990
But basic transportation is just is, is
the bike's core competency.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.570
And basic transportation is only one of
thousands of

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.910
big hairy problems we face in our world
today.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:02.030
Can we take what we've learned from the
bike and

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.270
apply it to these other issues as well?

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.600
I think so.
You know, here at Stanford,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.500
we're encouraged to tackle big problems
and change our world for the better.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:17.470
While inspiring, this mandate is also
daunting.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.580
I find myself wondering,

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.260
why am I more qualified than the many
brilliant minds that came before me?

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.080
Do I need signif, significant financial
resources to even begin?

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.140
I don't know about you, but

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:32.610
I often feel overwhelmed by the enormity
of our world's problems.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.170
Fix poverty, cure cancer, eradicate
malaria.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.000
I can feel my anxiety levels rising
already.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.040
My overwhelmed mind wants to give up
before I've even begun.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.129
The bike though has become my source of
inspiration.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:52.120
When I'm feeling overwhelmed by an
impossibly complicated task, I

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:56.590
think back to Belita and the impact that a
humble everyday bike has had on her life.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.440
I gain confidence knowing that incremental
solutions to some of Zambia's

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.020
worst problems, don't require a rocket
scientist or a billion dollars.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.530
The bike is a great example of how a
simple tool

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.150
can have a profound impact on our world.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.950
Maybe I am up to the task, and maybe you
are too.

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:18.900
I'll leave you with this.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:24.370
Sometimes we don't need billion dollar R
and D budgets to tackle big problems.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:27.010
All we need is creativity and a bit of
perspective.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:31.260
World Bicycle Relief is changing the world
through the power of the bicycle.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.980
What other everyday tools are we
overlooking?

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:40.480
Simple, sustainable solutions exist for
many of the world's most complex problems.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.721
In some cases, a meaningful solution may
be parked in your garage with

00:08:46.721 --> 00:08:48.937
a flat tire in a thin coat of dust.

00:08:48.937 --> 00:08:52.659
All you have to do is reimagine its
potential.

00:08:52.659 --> 00:08:53.402
Thank you.

00:08:53.402 --> 00:08:56.682
[APPLAUSE].

00:08:56.682 --> 00:09:00.869
[MUSIC]

